From: Cage contributors
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: drain translator locks before Wine kills peer threads

Wine invokes ProcessTerm around NtTerminateProcess(NULL), which kills peer
threads but returns to the calling thread for DLL/FLS cleanup. A peer killed
while writing a shared JIT lookup cache leaves its mutex permanently owned.
Cleanup can execute previously untranslated callbacks and block in FindBlock.

Take the existing locks in notification order, before peer termination, and
release them after the syscall (including failure). The exclusive invalidation
lock drains compilation and cache writers. No shutdown status is synthesized.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
@@ -716,7 +716,20 @@ NTSTATUS ProcessInit()
   return STATUS_SUCCESS;
 }
 
-void ProcessTerm(HANDLE Handle, BOOL After, NTSTATUS Status) {}
+void ProcessTerm(HANDLE Handle, BOOL After, NTSTATUS Status) {
+  if (!CTX) {
+    return;
+  }
+  // Wine calls this only for NtTerminateProcess(NULL): peers disappear, while
+  // this thread continues running DLL/FLS cleanup and may compile more code.
+  if (!After) {
+    ThreadCreationMutex.lock();
+    CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().lock();
+  } else {
+    CTX->GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
+    ThreadCreationMutex.unlock();
+  }
+}
 
 class ScopedCallbackDisable {
 private:
